5645 0 obj
<<
/Title (Molecular biology and genetics helped scientists understand the genes that encode light-sensitive proteins. By introducing the gene for ChR2 into neurons, researchers enabled these cells to respond to light, creating the foundation for optogenetic control.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 5629 0 R
/Next 5647 0 R
>>
endobj
5646 0 obj
<<
/Title (Neuroscience provided the knowledge needed to understand how neurons communicate and form circuits that control brain functions. Optogenetics allows researchers to activate or inhibit specific neurons with light, helping them investigate how these circuits contribute to behavior and brain activity.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 5647 0 R
/Next 5630 0 R
>>
endobj
5647 0 obj
<<
/Title (Microbiology and phycology contributed to the discovery of light-sensitive proteins in microorganisms and algae, particularly Chlamydomonas reinhardtii. Studying these proteins, including channelrhodopsins, inspired scientists to develop optogenetics as a tool for controlling the activity of neurons using light.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 5645 0 R
/Next 5646 0 R
>>
endobj
